We adopt `nocuous' from \citet{berry_kamsties_2004}, noting an inversion: where they describe \emph{divergent} human interpretations, our agents exhibit \emph{convergent} errors. Among ambiguous clauses, 41.3\% trigger majority-convergent violations ($\geq$3/5 models; 13.0\% unanimous)---a phenomenon we term \emph{systematic nocuity}: models converge on the same incorrect interpretation, invisible in agreement-based evaluation. Of these, 83.9\% share the dominant failure mode, confirming convergent---not coincident---violations. Incompleteness has the highest universal rate (24\%, OR $= 2.61$, $p = 0.019$; subject to the information-addition confound; \S\ref{sec:results}).

\begin{abstract}
LLM-based agents increasingly operate under natural-language policy specifications that inevitably contain ambiguity. We present a matched-pair causal study on $\tau^2$-bench with 300 ambiguous/unambiguous clause pairs spanning a six-type taxonomy, evaluated across five models. Ambiguous clauses are consistently nocuous (i.e., harmful), increasing violation rates by 34.0 percentage points under controlled synthetic conditions ($+$18.7 to $+$36.0\,pp excluding the confounded incompleteness type). Clause wording explains 22.4\% of violation variance versus 0.4\% for type; however, type predicts failure \emph{mechanism} an order of magnitude more strongly than violation rate, establishing the taxonomy as a diagnostic tool. Error analysis reveals disambiguation neglect ($\leq$11\% awareness) and \emph{systematic nocuity}: over 40\% of clauses triggering model-convergent violations. These results, observed in customer-service policy domains under synthetic ambiguity injection, motivate clause-level policy auditing and type-targeted diagnosis for policy-governed deployments.
\end{abstract}

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Experimental Platform}

We conduct all experiments on the original release of $\tau^2$-bench \citep{tau2_bench_2025}, a dual-control conversational agent benchmark spanning retail and airline customer service domains. $\tau^2$-bench provides natural-language policy documents, tool APIs, and multi-turn user interactions with ground-truth evaluation via assertion checking. Its policy injection mechanism allows us to replace individual policy clauses while preserving the surrounding context, enabling controlled ambiguity manipulation.

\subsection{Matched-Pair Design}

For each of the six ambiguity types, we construct 50 \emph{clause pairs}: an ambiguous variant and a matched unambiguous variant that conveys the same intended policy but resolves the ambiguity. Pairs are minimal edits: the unambiguous variant adds only the information necessary to eliminate the target ambiguity (e.g., for scopal ambiguity, replacing ``items may be returned within 30 days'' with ``standard items may be returned within 30 days; customized items are final sale''). This yields 300 clause pairs $\times$ 2 conditions $\times$ 5 models $= 2{,}997$ episodes (3 episodes lost to API errors in one Qwen3 batch).

Clause pairs are generated through a template-based pipeline and reviewed by two independent annotators (both with NLP background) to ensure (1) the ambiguous variant contains exactly one instance of the target type, (2) the unambiguous variant resolves it without changing policy scope, and (3) the ambiguity is plausibly natural. Annotators independently verified type assignments with a 96\% initial agreement (287/300; Cohen's $\kappa = 0.95$ on six-type classification); the 13 disagreements were resolved by discussion, yielding 100\% final consensus. A post-hoc realism validation confirms linguistic consistency with original $\tau^2$-bench policy text (\S\ref{sec:limitations}).

\subsection{Models}

We evaluate five models spanning four providers and two architecture families: GPT-5.4 and GPT-4.1 (OpenAI, proprietary dense), Claude Sonnet 4.6 (Anthropic, proprietary dense), Qwen3-235B (Alibaba, open-source Mixture-of-Experts), and DeepSeek-V3 (DeepSeek, open-source dense). This selection covers frontier and mid-tier proprietary models alongside open-source alternatives with different training philosophies. All API calls use temperature $= 0$, top\_p $= 1$, and max\_tokens $= 4096$, and were made on May 20, 2026.

\subsection{Evaluation}

We use a cross-judge design to mitigate self-evaluation bias: GPT-5.4 judges Claude, GPT-4.1, and Qwen3 traces; GPT-4.1 judges GPT-5.4 and DeepSeek traces. Because our matched-pair design compares ambiguous and unambiguous conditions within the same model--judge pairing, any systematic judge bias cancels in the treatment effect ($\Delta$pp); the cross-judge asymmetry affects only absolute violation rates, not C1 or C2 (\S\ref{sec:limitations}). Judges classify each episode outcome on a four-tier severity scale: none, minor, moderate, and critical. Our primary analysis uses moderate-or-above severity as the violation threshold; minor-only deviations (6.6\% of episodes) are excluded. Including minor violations (overall rate 49.6\% vs.\ 41.0\%) produces even larger effects without changing any directional conclusion.

\paragraph{Cross-judge calibration.} To quantify potential judge bias, we conducted a dual-judge calibration on 100 stratified episodes (60 from GPT-5.4, 40 from other models; 65\% ambiguous).\footnote{The calibration sample stratifies toward ambiguous episodes (65\%) to ensure adequate violation events for $\kappa$ estimation; binary $\kappa$ is condition-agnostic.} Both judges evaluated each episode independently. McNemar's test reveals no systematic detection bias ($p = 0.606$; GPT-4.1-only violations: 9, GPT-5.4-only: 6). Binary agreement is substantial ($\kappa = 0.653$), with weighted four-tier severity agreement reaching $\kappa_w = 0.775$. Per-type binary agreement ranges from $\kappa = 0.385$ (authorization scope) to $0.875$ (incompleteness); severity-level divergence does not affect core conclusions since our primary analyses use binary thresholds (Appendix~\ref{app:severity}).

\paragraph{Human validation.} Two annotators (NLP background), blind to LLM labels and condition, labeled 100 stratified episodes on the four-tier scale. Inter-annotator agreement is substantial ($\kappa = 0.798$, $\kappa_w = 0.762$). Pooled human--LLM agreement ($\kappa = 0.733$, $\kappa_w = 0.807$; per-type breakdown in Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_agreement}) approaches inter-annotator levels, validating the automated pipeline \citep{zheng_llm_judge_2024}. This validation supports pooled pipeline reliability; per-type estimates ($n \approx 32$--$34$) have wide confidence intervals and should not be interpreted as type-level reliability guarantees.

\subsection{Controls}

\paragraph{Perturbation control.} To confirm specificity to semantic ambiguity, we run 300 episodes with non-ambiguity perturbations (synonym substitution, clause reordering). The perturbation effect is $\Delta_{\text{perturb}} = -0.67$ pp (95\% CI $[-8.0, 6.67]$, containing zero). Even under the worst-case CI bound (6.67 pp), the residual attributable to ambiguity is $\geq$27.3 pp. This control tests surface-level perturbation specificity; the information-addition confound specific to incompleteness is addressed separately via logistic regression (Appendix~\ref{app:confound}).

\paragraph{Reproducibility.} A replication of 30 clause pairs $\times$ 2 models $\times$ 3 replicates yields ICC $= 0.919$ (95\% CI $[0.892, 0.940]$). ICC is computed within the OpenAI family; cross-provider stability is supported by C1 consistency across all seven models.

\subsection{Statistical Analysis}

We use McNemar's test for paired binary outcomes, $\chi^2$ tests with Cram\'{e}r's $V$ for omnibus effects, Fisher's exact test for per-type comparisons, and TOST equivalence testing \citep{lakens_2017} with a primary SESOI of $\pm 15$ pp. We report the matched-pair odds ratio from McNemar's test on discordant outcomes ($n_{\text{disc}} = 580$ of 1{,}497 pairs), which conditions on within-pair disagreement and thus differs from the crude marginal odds ratio. No industry-standard SESOI exists for LLM agent safety evaluation. Our $\pm 15$\,pp threshold satisfies two principled criteria \citep{lakens_2017}: it exceeds measurement noise (perturbation control upper CI: 6.67\,pp) by $>$2$\times$, and a per-type difference below this margin does not warrant distinct mitigation strategies. We acknowledge this margin is statistically motivated rather than anchored in deployment incident data, which does not yet exist for LLM agent policy violations; as the field matures, production violation baselines may provide domain-specific calibration. Table~\ref{tab:tost_sensitivity} reports sensitivity across seven margins \citep{anvari_lakens_2021}; the margin-free bootstrap cluster analysis (\S\ref{sec:results}) provides a threshold-independent complement. We apply Holm--Bonferroni correction within three pre-registered test families (Appendix~\ref{app:multiple_comparison}); post-hoc power exceeds 0.99 for C1 and C2.

